5 "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192920 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186193647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192920 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192920 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192920 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "02255835-1ac4-5d6f-b968-e337f6d42745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193647 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193647 #edb/ref 1061 41649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193647 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "93a1b04d-d02e-5aae-bd94-6c2a571d80d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193647 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193648 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193648 #edb/ref 1061 41650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193648 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f42bb3f4-1e7f-53f5-ad85-ca2e942d30b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193648 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193649 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193649 #edb/ref 1061 41653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193649 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c579fcb-af17-53b9-89c4-b7c4d86d6a8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193649 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193650 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193650 #edb/ref 1061 41665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193650 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "68866355-7603-545f-b26a-1a0cee48c958" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193650 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193651 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193651 #edb/ref 1061 41670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193651 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "18e8a405-fc8d-5344-9133-d42b106e46e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193651 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193652 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193652 #edb/ref 1061 41676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193652 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "40d83f58-34e5-5b7b-b300-54ae33e99a59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193652 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193653 #edb/ref 1061 41677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193653 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fda08207-59ef-5481-816d-d4abed842b3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193653 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193876 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that $H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nIn the vector space $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ there are two **trivial subspaces**.\n\n- One trivial subspace is the set \n\n$$\nH=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}\n$$\n\n consisting of just the zero vector. This is a subspace because the sum of two zero vectors is the zero vector, and any multiple of the zero vector is the zero vector.\n\n- Another trivial subspace is the set \n\n$$\nH = \\mathbb{R}^n\n$$\n\n consisting of the entire set $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is a subspace because the sum of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ and any multiple of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193876 #edb/ref 1065 6854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193876 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "01ed4d92-df35-5f3c-aa41-43246f6ea9e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193876 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193877 #edb/ref 1064 "In $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n*The only non-trivial subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ are straight lines that pass through the origin.*\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows a subspace in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c0ea9584ae3229a7930c6ef2b53031770a7190b980b5b595cc233cae29ea49d.png)\n\nLet's now consider a few examples of subsets of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ that are *not* subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\n![](images/5a/5aac9808e9196653ee54974b495443a95d8913cd0b220a6893acb68ff3b967bb.png)\n\n- Diagram I shows a line that does not pass through the origin. It is *not* a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Diagram II is not a subspace because $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf v$ belong to the set while $\\mathbf u+\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under vector addition.\n\n- Diagram III is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $-\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under scalar multiplication." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193877 #edb/ref 1065 6857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193877 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "33c42a7a-a5c2-5b36-a839-4fab6351c9e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193877 #edb/ref 1067 "Non-Trivial Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193878 #edb/ref 1064 "In $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n*The only non-trivial subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ are straight lines or planes that pass through the origin.*\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n![](images/57/57b6625b607537d1c90e849d6ee6ac8480648aa4ad6e3a357bedc8d8408256ad.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the sets shown in the diagrams below are *not* subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4fbaee302e1c25e740f4164841a674243a43197b0f20ddb35d2d41bb4175b68.png)\n\n- Diagram I shows a plane that does not pass through the origin. It is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Diagram II shows a line that does not pass through the origin. It is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193878 #edb/ref 1065 6861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193878 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8d9802f0-c392-5e37-afb4-72f73451813e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193878 #edb/ref 1067 "Non-Trivial Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193879 #edb/ref 1064 "Lastly, consider the set $\\mathbb{R},$ which belongs to the family of vector spaces $\\mathbb{R}^n$ (when $n=1$). This vector space has only two subspaces, both of which are trivial:\n\n- the zero subspace $\\{0\\},$ which contains only the number $0,$ and\n\n- the whole space $\\mathbb{R}$ (or, formally, $\\mathbb{R}^1$), which contains all the real numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193879 #edb/ref 1065 6862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193879 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fb0671eb-0c5d-59dc-a3f9-cda72b4da034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193879 #edb/ref 1067 "Final Notes About the Set of Real Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244850 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244850 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244850 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3d3026ef-0c9f-56f6-9c82-deeb31d5c484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245613 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245613 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245613 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9497acc2-e957-5d08-b023-f36e5c982edf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245911 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245911 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245911 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b34b7ad9-ca5d-5d2e-8c18-a98455f051ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245973 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245973 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245973 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b9e6cab8-baf8-54e3-aa8d-7bdaa5531b01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246290 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246290 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246290 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "def3c3d6-726e-5345-9da6-7f08cc46817e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246673 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186244850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246673 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186245613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246673 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186245911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246673 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186245973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246673 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186246290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246673 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246673 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246673 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186246290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246673 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4c72e079-6317-5a21-9e66-00a4c7adfda0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247159 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247159 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110760" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247159 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247159 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x-4y=1\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x=-2y\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}<0\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247159 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "49bd226a-4547-5889-a62c-e6c2912a1520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247159 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each subset.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. For instance, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $x-4y=1$ since $0-4\\cdot 0=0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=-2y$ since $0=-2\\cdot 0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. No point in ${R}^{3}$ satisfies the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}<0.$ Hence, $C=∅.$ The empty set is not a subspace since it does not contain the zero vector.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272574 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272574 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272574 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14ca2bb0-45fa-515e-86a8-cce64866690b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273219 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273219 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273219 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "604d7a59-1fef-523e-bf07-a9f5dd400e7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273310 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273310 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273310 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6afe9e0d-cdbd-5294-ac31-28a3a86f03a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273808 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273808 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273808 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a4a6743f-b156-512b-8812-e7ac6baca636" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274365 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274365 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274365 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dfde1b99-be1a-5fba-9ccf-784956f7918d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274832 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186272574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274832 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274832 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274832 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186273808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274832 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186274365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274832 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274832 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274832 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186274365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274832 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "73ce813a-1598-5c11-b720-fcf588d7f65b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275442 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186274832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275442 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158101" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275442 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275442 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/27/2739a0225640ff20cc9678edf8c41ba45ff637b17a40d0322ca792bef1346f06.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275442 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ce01e6e-4d84-5a03-940c-8de7146f3c76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275442 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315208 #edb/ref 1080 "None" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315208 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315208 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ef4ac66-d953-5fba-ac6a-17597fb2834c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315349 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315349 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315349 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "28317ab6-956f-51f4-9c07-7893dc4262d4" #